<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Strata's privacy manifest.

    NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes IS DELIBERATELY EMPTY, and that is not an
    oversight. Apple's definition, verbatim from the App Privacy Details page:

        "Collect" refers to transmitting data off the device in a way that
        allows you and/or your third-party partners to access it for a period
        longer than what is necessary to service the transmitted request in
        real time. ... Data that is processed only on device is not "collected"
        and does not need to be disclosed.

    Strata transmits nothing. Wins live in SwiftData on the device, photographs
    live in the app's own container, and there is no server, no account, no
    analytics and no third-party SDK in the project. The one network call in
    the app is CLGeocoder asking Apple what a coordinate is called, which is a
    real-time request Apple services and we never retain.

    This file used to declare HealthAndFitness and PhotosOrVideos. Both were
    wrong, and wrong in the direction that hurts: Apple builds the App Store
    product page's privacy label from what is declared here, so it was telling
    people Strata collects their health data and their photographs when it
    collects neither and there is no HealthKit code left in the app at all.
    A privacy label is a statement to users, and an untrue one is worse than a
    missing one.

    If a server, an account, a backup or an analytics SDK is ever added, this
    is the first file to change and the App Store Connect answers are the
    second — they have to agree.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<!--
		    UserDefaults, for the app's own settings: the timer length, whether
		    photographs go to the camera roll, whether wins remember where they
		    were. CA92.1 is "accessing information from the app itself", which
		    is the whole of it — there is no app group and no managed
		    configuration.

		    Nothing else in the app touches a required-reason API. Audited:
		    no file-timestamp calls, no disk-space calls, no system-boot-time
		    calls, no active-keyboard calls.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
